{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MDbV4G8RR3yhX8yj3EAjyX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a Go project that re-implements Unix utilities from specifications derived from C source code. The pipeline is: extract specs from C → write PRDs → generate Go code → verify with differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge from reference behavior on edge cases.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/testutils/` - differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - platform-specific syscall abstractions\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - shared output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - build targets\n- `docs/` - specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current state**: The testutils, sys, and format packages are already implemented with their respective modules and tests, and the cat utility has been started in the cmd directory.\n\n**Existing commands**: wc and version are complete with tests, while sponge, du, and ts haven't been implemented yet.\n\n**Release roadmap**: The foundational packages (magefiles, format, sys, testutils) are spec-complete but not yet started, followed by wc in the next release, then cat, and sponge after that.\n\n**Key discrepancy**: The source code shows actual implementations for cat, wc, and the utility packages, but the code_status document marks them as \"not started\" — I should trust what's actually in the source code over the documentation. Looking at the release roadmap, I can see that rel00.0 through rel01.0 have all their required components in place, but rel01.1 is missing the test file for cat, and rel01.2 and rel01.3 have no source code at all yet.\n\nSince I can only propose one task and need to follow the release-driven priority, the earliest incomplete work is the cat tests for rel01.1 — the implementation exists but the test coverage is missing. Looking at the PRD requirements for cat from the source code, I can see it needs to handle file concatenation with various options like line numbering, blank line squeezing, and display formatting for non-printing characters. The test file should mirror the structure of wc_test.go and cover these requirements systematically.\n\nFor a differential test task limited to 4 requirements and around 150-250 lines, I'll group the PRD requirements into testable chunks: basic file concatenation and stdin handling, line numbering with the various flag combinations, blank line squeezing, and display options for special characters. The reference binary would be `gcat` from GNU coreutils via Homebrew, and I'll use the same TestMain setup with testutils.DiffTest to compare outputs between the reference and Go implementations. and file handling (prd006-cat R1.1-R1.4)\n- R2: Test line numbering flags -n and -b (prd006-cat R2.1-R2.3)\n- R3: Test display transformation flags -s, -E, -T, -v, -A, -e, -t (prd006-cat R3.1-R3.2, R4.1-R4.9)\n- R4: Test error handling and edge cases (prd006-cat R5.2, R5.3)\n\nThat's 4 requirements. Good.\n\nNow let me write the task description.\n\nFor required_reading, the agent needs:\n- cmd/cat/main.go (the implementation to test)\n- pkg/testutils/difftest.go (the differential testing harness)\n- cmd/wc/wc_test.go (reference pattern for differential tests)\n\nI need to clarify the scope constraints. The excluded paths from DD7 only restrict what I can propose creating or modifying—docs/ and other excluded directories can still be referenced in required_reading for context. Since this task creates cmd/cat/cat_test.go, which is in the cmd/ directory, it's within scope. which is in cmd/ — that's fine.\n\nLet me also check: does a test suite spec exist for cat? The analysis shows rel01.1-uc001-cat has specstatus: spec_complete. There should be a test suite at docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-01.1.yaml. I should include that in required_reading.\n\nActually, I don't have the PRD file content. I need to reference it for the agent. Let me include the PRD in required_reading so the agent reads it.\n\nLet me write the task:\n\nTitle: \"prd006-cat R1-R5 -- differential tests for cmd/cat\"\n\nFiles:\n- cmd/cat/cat_test.go (create)\n\nRequired reading:\n- cmd/cat/main.go (implementation being tested)\n- cmd/wc/wc_test.go (reference pattern for differential tests)\n- pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests API)\n- docs/specs/product-requirements/prd006-cat.yaml (requirements being tested)\n- docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-01.1.yaml (test cases to implement)\n\nRequirements (max 4):\n- R1: Test file concatenation — default mode with stdin, named files, multiple files in order, and \"-\" argument (prd006-cat R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4)\n- R2: Test line numbering — -n numbers all lines, -b numbers non-blank lines, -b overrides -n (prd006-cat R2.1, R2.2, R2.3)\n- R3: Test display transformation flags — -s squeezes blank lines, -A shows all special characters, -E shows line endings, -T shows tabs (prd006-cat R3.1, R3.2, R3.3, R3.4)\n- R4: Test error handling — missing file reports error to stderr and exits 1, mix of valid and invalid files processes valid ones (prd006-cat R5.2)\n\nDesign decisions:\n- D1: Follow the exact pattern in cmd/wc/wc_test.go: TestMain builds Go binary and locates GNU reference binary (gcat), tests use testutils.DiffTest and testutils.RunDiffTests\n- D2: Reference binary is \"gcat\" (GNU cat installed via Homebrew)\n- D3: Group test functions by PRD requirement area: TestDefault, TestLineNumbering, TestDisplayTransformations, TestErrorHandling\n\nI'm finalizing the acceptance criteria to ensure the test suite covers all the requirements comprehensively.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input